# Base packages for nox on Fedora.
#
# Per-distribution data (NULL.md §9.1): the names genuinely differ between
# distributions and no abstraction can fix that. Install with:
#     bin/pkg install-list base
#
# Every entry says why it is here. A package list without reasons is
# unreviewable a year later, which is when it matters.

# --- the font ----------------------------------------------------------
# The whole design rests on one bitmap typeface (NULL.md I2). The console
# build carries the PSF strikes the ramp is derived from and the virtual
# console uses; terminus-fonts serves the rest through fontconfig as .otb --
# which is BITMAP STRIKES IN AN OPENTYPE WRAPPER, not an outline build. It has
# strikes at 12,14,16,18,20,22,24,28,32 px and nothing in between.
#
# Measured, not assumed (verify/check-font-substitution.py): both WebKit and
# Gecko do render it -- so §8.10's "a bitmap font will not render in a browser
# engine" is not what happens here. What happens is worse to detect: they
# SCALE it. Widths come out exactly proportional to the requested size, so at
# any size that is not a strike the text is a scaled bitmap, which is the one
# thing this design exists to avoid (§9.3).
#
# Hence the TTF build, for contexts that cannot be pinned to a strike. Same
# design, same 4.49 series, main Fedora repository, OFL-1.1.
terminus-fonts                  # ter-u18n etc. (.otb strikes), via fontconfig
terminus-fonts-console          # ter-112n / ter-118n PSF, in /usr/lib/kbd/consolefonts/
ax86-terminus-ttf-fonts         # outline build, for browser engines (§8.10)

# --- the compositor ----------------------------------------------------
# Settled at Phase 0 against §8.1's six requirements. Hyprland is NOT
# packaged for Fedora and must not be used here -- it would come from a
# third-party repository, which §9.1 forbids for a core component.
sway

# --- GPU / bake --------------------------------------------------------
# The simulation is a Vulkan compute workload (§5.5). NVK exposes the
# discrete adapter, so no proprietary driver is required; this was verified
# at Phase 0 rather than assumed.
mesa-vulkan-drivers
vulkan-tools                    # vulkaninfo: confirms which adapters exist

# --- renderer build dependencies ---------------------------------------
# The renderer is a Wayland layer-shell client (§6.3). libxkbcommon is not
# optional even for surfaces that take no keyboard: the toolkit links it, and
# the column needs it for real (§7.3), since a keycode is not a character and
# only the layout says which.
rust
cargo
wayland-devel
libxkbcommon-devel

# --- components (§8.4) --------------------------------------------------
# Each is named because a component needs it, not because it is nice to have.
fzf                 # the filter-picker every menu is (§7.4)
btop                # the monitor the column hosts (§10.5 decided this)
foot                # a terminal that renders bitmap fonts correctly
fd-find             # `find` re-runs this per keystroke; the picker's own matcher is off
brightnessctl       # the backlight, which settings reads and writes rather than owning
dunst               # the notification daemon the on-screen display uses (§7.5).
                    # Not mako: dunst already owns the bus name here, and a
                    # second daemon simply cannot take it.
grim slurp          # capture
wl-clipboard        # clipboard, and putting a capture's path where it can be pasted

# --- capture ------------------------------------------------------------
# §8.4: capture is both halves in one place -- stills and recordings.
# grim and slurp are already pulled in by the compositor spin.
wf-recorder                     # screen recording on wlroots

# --- the last three components (§8.4) ---------------------------------
# playerctl is already pulled in by the bar; these are the two that were not.
cliphist                        # clipboard history, for the clipboard component
cava                            # the audio spectrum in the column (§10.7 warns
                                # this dependency can multiply a surface's cost;
                                # it is measured in docs/measurements.md)

# The bake's Python dependencies. Absent from this list until a clean machine
# tried to run it: numpy for the simulation and the quantiser, pillow for the
# boot surfaces, gobject for the icon theme. They are on nox because something
# else pulled them in, which is exactly why they were never noticed missing.
python3-numpy
python3-pillow
python3-gobject
